    "id": "art_triad-crossing-the-sets",
    "slug": "triad-crossing-the-sets",
    "title": "Changing Register Without Moving Your Hand",
    "summary": "Neighbouring string sets overlap enough that one hand position holds C major in four registers, and a progression can jump sets mid-phrase for free because the fret movement is identical on every set.",
    "tags": ["triads", "chords", "fretboard"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Any two neighbouring string sets share two of their three strings, which is why a chord is never more than a set away from where your hand already is. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The open C chord",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-open-chords-you-know" }
        },
        {
          "text": " already proved this: it holds three grips at once, on three overlapping sets, in all three inversions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the same fact stated as a single hand position. Play "
        },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " major on each of the four sets without moving out of the first three frets, and the four grips span almost two octaves of the same three note names, "
        },
        { "text": "G2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " up to " },
        { "text": "E4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": [
        [{ "text": "Set" }],
        [{ "text": "Inversion" }],
        [{ "text": "Chart" }],
