    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The hand moves about two frets between the second and third chords, and the register jumps with it. The top note of the "
        },
        { "text": "F", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " grip is " },
        { "text": "A3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and the bottom note of the " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " grip is " },
        { "text": "B3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", so the whole second half sits entirely above the whole first half, even though the hand barely moved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Finding a crossing point is mostly two habits. Decide which note you want lowest, and take the set that puts it there. Or, on the neck itself, remember that any two neighbouring sets share two strings, so a grip and its neighbour occupy overlapping ground rather than distant territory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "If the set decides the register and the inversion decides where the hand sits, what does a listener actually pick out of all that?"
        }
      ]
    }
